:pnlgrp.
.************************************************************************
.*  Help for command CLOPIPE
.************************************************************************
:help name='CLOPIPE'.
Close Pipe to Unix Cmd - Help
:p.The Close Pipe to Unix Cmd (CLOPIPE) command stops a QShell/PASE
process that was opened with the OPNPIPE command, and closes up
the pipes that were connected to it.
:p.When the QShell/PASE process is stopped, the CLOPIPE command
checks to see if the command exited normally or abnormally. If it
ended abnormally, CLOPIPE reports that error to it's caller.
.*
:p.:hp2.Restrictions::ehp2.
:ul.
:li.
You must previously open a pipe with the OPNPIPE command
before you run this.
:li.
See the help for the OPNPIPE command for information about
the authorities needed to use this suite of tools.
:eul.
:ehelp.
.*******************************************
.*   Help for parameter PIPEID
.*******************************************
:help name='CLOPIPE/PIPEID'.
Pipe ID to close (PIPEID) - Help
:xh3.Pipe ID to close (PIPEID)
:p.Tells the CLOPIPE command which pipe to close. This is
particularly useful if you have more than one pipe open
simultaneously.
:parml.
:pt.:pk def.*DFT:epk.
:pd.
The default pipe is closed. The default pipe is the one opened
most recently by the OPNPIPE command.
:pt.:pv.decimal-number:epv.
:pd.
Specify the Pipe ID of an open pipe as returned from the OPNPIPE
command.
:eparml.
:ehelp.
.**************************************************
.*
.* Examples for CLOPIPE
.*
.**************************************************
:help name='CLOPIPE/COMMAND/EXAMPLES'.
Examples for CLOPIPE - Help
:xh3.Examples for CLOPIPE
:p.:hp2.Example 1: Simple Command Example:ehp2.
:xmp.
CLOPIPE
:exmp.
:p.The most recently opened pipe is closed.
.*
:p.:hp2.Example 2: More Complex Command Example:ehp2.
:xmp.
OPNPIPE CMD('ls -l') PIPEID(&MYPIPE)
/* do other stuff here */
CLOPIPE PIPEID(&MYPIPE)
:exmp.
:p.The OPNPIPE command runs the Unix 'ls -l' command in
a background process connected to a pipe. OPNPIPE sets the &MYPIPE
variable to contain an ID number that identifies the open pipe.
:p.The CLOPIPE command closes the pipe identified by the &MYPIPE
variable that was returned from OPNPIPE.
:ehelp.
.**************************************************
.*
.* Error messages for CLOPIPE
.*
.**************************************************
:help name='CLOPIPE/ERROR/MESSAGES'.
&msg(CPX0005,QCPFMSG). CLOPIPE - Help
:xh3.&msg(CPX0005,QCPFMSG). CLOPIPE
